  "query_preview": "/MONDAY THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nIssued at 145 PM CST Fri Nov 13 2026\n\nUpper ridging begins to amplify across the central Plains as we head \ninto early next week, with deep upper troughing noted across the \nwestern US. This brings a return of southwesterly flow aloft by \nTuesday, and with it temperatures moderating back to above average \nby midweek. Wednesday looks to be the warmest of the week, as warm \nadvection further strengthens ahead of an approaching cold front. \nThis looks to boost highs into the upper 50s to lower 60s, with the \nwarmest temps east of HWY 61. The aforementioned cold front is in \nresponse to the first of a pair of upper lows, as it ejects \nnortheast out of the Rockies and an associated surface low moves \nacross the Dakotas. This could lead to light rain north of HWY 2, \nthough confidence in this remains low for now. \n\nGuidance continues to hint at another system approaching the area by \nlate week and into next weekend, though solutions diverge greatly \nwith respect to both track and strength. This lends very low \nconfidence, especially with timing and amounts of any associated \nprecipitation. That said, this does look to be the next shot at any \nbeneficial moisture locally, along with some threat for strong \nwinds, and will continue to be monitored closely. Until details \nbecome more clear, amounts and precipitation types will remain \nuncertain late week across western and north central Nebraska.",
